          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
          #Factorisation.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Cof
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Cof}
              of small categories and retrofunctors has an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+factorization+system"} orthogonal factorization system
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Bij
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi DOpf
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Bij^{op}, DOpf)
              which factors each retrofunctor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↛
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi : A \nrightarrow B
              into a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijective+on+objects+functor"} bijective on objects functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ←
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \leftarrow I
              followed by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibration"} discrete opfibration
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \to B
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              See (
              %a{:href => "#Garner19"}> Garner 2019, Proposition 5
              )
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Poly
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Poly}(1, 1)
              be the monoidal category arising from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+functor"} bicategory of polynomials
              on the singleton set. Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Cof
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Cof}
              is isomorphic to the category of comonoids in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Poly
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Poly}(1, 1)
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Originally proven in (
              %a{:href => "#AhmanUustalu16"}> Ahman-Uustalu 2016
              ). See (
              %a{:href => "#SpivakNiu21"}> Spivak-Niu 2021, Theorem 6.26
              ) for details.
          %p
            A comonoid in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Poly
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Poly}(1, 1)
            is also known as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+comonad"} polynomial comonad
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
                yields a retrofunctor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi disc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↛
                      %mi disc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} disc(A) \nrightarrow disc(B)
                between
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi Set
                        %mo →
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi Cof
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Set} \to \mathbf{Cof}
            %li
              %p
                Every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
                yields a retrofunctor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo ↛
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \nrightarrow A
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi Mon
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Cof
                          %mi op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Mon} \to \mathbf{Cof}^{op}
            %li
              %p
                Every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijective+on+objects+functor"} bijective on objects functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
                yields a retrofunctor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo ↛
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \nrightarrow A
            %li
              %p
                Every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibration"} discrete opfibration
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
                yields a retrofunctor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ↛
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \nrightarrow B
            %li
              %p
                Every split
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck opfibration
                has an underlying retrofunctor given by the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cleavage"} splitting
            %li
              %p
                More generally, every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta+lens"} delta lens
                has an underlying retrofunctor.
            %li
              %p
                Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                denote the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
                under addition. A retrofunctor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau : A \to \mathbb{N}
                is the same as a choice of morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau(a, 1)
                out of every object in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comorphism"} comorphism
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal
                retrofunctor in the category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
                of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifolds
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#HigginsMackenzie93"} Higgins-Mackenzie 1993, definition 5.12
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The notion of retrofunctor first appeared under the name
            %em comorphism
            in the paper:
          %ul
            %li#HigginsMackenzie93
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+J.+Higgins"}> Philip J. Higgins
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kirill+C.+H.+Mackenzie"} Kirill C. H. Mackenzie
              = succeed "," do
                %em Duality for base-changing morphisms of vector bundles, modules, Lie algebroids and Poisson structures
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1017/S0305004100071760"} doi:10.1017/S0305004100071760
          %p
            The definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal
            retrofunctor between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category+in+a+monoidal+category"} non-cartesian internal categories
            was introduced in Section 4.4 of the thesis:
          %ul
            %li#Aguiar97
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcelo+Aguiar"}> Marcelo Aguiar
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Internal categories and quantum groups
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://pi.math.cornell.edu/~maguiar/thesis2.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The characterization of retrofunctors as morphisms between directed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+functor"} containers
            is developed in the papers:
          %ul
            %li#AhmanUustalu16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Danel+Ahman"}> Danel Ahman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarmo+Uustalu"} Tarmo Uustalu
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Directed Containers as Categories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.4204/EPTCS.207.5"} doi:10.4204/EPTCS.207.5
            %li#AhmanUustalu17
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Danel+Ahman"}> Danel Ahman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarmo+Uustalu"} Tarmo Uustalu
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Taking updates seriously
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://ceur-ws.org/Vol-1827/paper11.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The link between retrofunctors,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta+lenses"} delta lenses
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck opfibrations
            is developed in the papers:
          %ul
            %li#Clarke20a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bryce+Clarke"}> Bryce Clarke
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Internal lenses as functors and cofunctors
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.4204/EPTCS.323.13"} doi:10.4204/EPTCS.323.13
            %li#Clarke20b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bryce+Clarke"}> Bryce Clarke
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Internal split opfibrations and cofunctors
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/35/44/35-44abs.html"} link
          %p
            Retrofunctors between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoids
            and the link with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+automorphism"} inner automorphisms
            of groupoids is explored in the paper:
          %ul
            %li#Garner19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"}> Richard Garner
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Inner automorphisms of groupoids
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.10378"} arXiv:1907.10378
          %p
            The notion of retrofunctor between partite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal categories
            is introduced in Definition 5.5 of the paper:
          %ul
            %li#CockettGarner20
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robin+Cockett"}> Robin Cockett
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"} Richard Garner
              = succeed "," do
                %em Generalising the étale groupoid–complete pseudogroup correspondence
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.09699"} arXiv:2004.09699
          %p A detailed account of the relationship between retrofunctors and polynomials appears in Chapter 6 of the draft textbook:
          %ul
            %li#SpivakNiu21
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Spivak"}> David Spivak
              , Nelson Niu,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Polynomial Functors: A General Theory of Interaction
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://topos.site/poly-book.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Enrichment in the category of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Cof
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Cof}
            of categories and retrofunctors is considered in the paper:
          %ul
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord>
                Kristopher Brown
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Kristopher+Brown"} ?
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+I.+Spivak"} David I. Spivak
              = succeed "," do
                %em Dynamic Tracing: a graphical language for rewriting protocols
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.14950"} arXiv:2304.14950
          %p
            The terminology
            %em retrofunctor
            was introduced in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthew+Di+Meglio"}> Matthew Di Meglio
              ,
              = succeed "." do
                %em The category of asymmetric lenses and its proxy pullbacks
              %a{:href => "https://mdimeglio.github.io/papers/The_category_of_asymmetric_lenses_and_its_proxy_pullbacks.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The terminology is justified by the fact that retrofunctors are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+retromorphisms"} monad retromorphisms
            in the double category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Span
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Span(Set)
        .revisedby
